[2026-04-08 07:26:22] [pre-recon] [LLM] Turn 180: {"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01RDQEFNd7XU9W3NGgPnUHwF","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"SSRF/External Request Tracer","prompt":"You are a security specialist focused on Server-Side Request Forgery (SSRF) vulnerabilities. Analyze this Node.js Express application (HireFlow/WebVulnBench).\n\n**CONTEXT:** This is a Node.js Express backend serving a freelance marketplace. Key files are in src/ with modules: auth, users, gigs, contracts, payments, messaging, reviews, disputes, notifications, admin, integrations, middleware, utils, config, models. It uses puppeteer for PDF generation, nodemailer for email, minio for object storage, and has webhook/integration endpoints.\n\n**Tasks:**\n1. Read src/integrations/webhook.service.js COMPLETELY - analyze:\n   - testWebhook function: URL validation, request details, response handling\n   - importProfile function: URL validation, what data is fetched and how it's processed\n   - processPaymentWebhook: signature verification bypass\n2. Read src/messaging/messaging.service.js - analyze the fetchLinkPreview function:\n   - URL validation/blocklist implementation\n   - Bypass techniques for the blocklist\n   - What data from the response is returned to the user\n3. Read src/utils/pdf.js - analyze Puppeteer usage:\n   - Can user data influence page.goto or setContent?\n   - Can HTML injection in rendered content cause SSRF via Puppeteer?\n4. Check src/config/index.js for MinIO, database, and external service configurations\n5. Search for any other HTTP client usage (axios, fetch, http.request, https.request, got, node-fetch, urllib) across the entire codebase\n6. Check for DNS resolution, URL parsing that could be exploited\n7. Look for redirect handlers in all route files\n8. Check nodemailer configuration for SSRF via SMTP\n\n**For EACH SSRF sink found, provide:**\n- Exact file path with line numbers\n- The vulnerable code snippet\n- What user input controls the URL/request\n- What URL validation exists (if any)\n- Specific bypass techniques\n- What internal services are reachable (postgres:5432, mongodb:27017, redis:6379, minio:9000, mailhog:1025)\n- Whether response data is returned to the attacker\n- Severity and attack scenario\n\nFocus on network-reachable endpoints only (accessible via HTTP API).","subagent_type":"general-purpose"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}
