[2026-04-08 07:26:31] [pre-recon] [LLM] Turn 200: {"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Qiv2Yk3NHg5o5n2YXhuEQq","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"Data Security Auditor Agent","prompt":"You are a data security specialist. Analyze this Node.js freelance marketplace application (HireFlow/WebVulnBench) for data security issues.\n\n**CONTEXT:** This app uses PostgreSQL (via Knex.js), MongoDB (via Mongoose), Redis (for sessions), and MinIO (for file storage). The application handles financial data (wallets, escrow, transactions), PII (emails, phone numbers, addresses), and user-generated content.\n\n**Tasks:**\n1. Read migrations/20240101000000_initial_schema.js - analyze the complete DB schema for:\n   - Sensitive data columns (passwords, tokens, PII, financial data)\n   - Missing constraints or checks\n   - Encryption at rest\n2. Read src/models/ directory - analyze Mongoose schemas for:\n   - Data validation\n   - Sensitive field exposure\n   - Index security\n3. Read src/payments/payments.service.js - analyze:\n   - Wallet operations (deposit, withdraw, escrow)\n   - Transaction integrity\n   - Race conditions in financial operations\n   - Amount validation\n4. Read src/config/index.js - analyze database connection security:\n   - SSL/TLS settings\n   - Authentication configuration\n   - Connection string security\n5. Read seeds/ directory - check for seeded test data with real-looking credentials\n6. Read mongo_seed.js - check for sensitive seeded data\n7. Search for encryption/hashing utilities in src/utils/\n8. Check how PII is handled in user endpoints (masking, filtering)\n9. Analyze Redis session storage security\n10. Check MinIO bucket configurations and access policies\n11. Look at how reset tokens and verification tokens are stored\n\n**Output Format:** Provide detailed analysis of:\n- Database schema security (with specific table/column concerns)\n- Financial data integrity controls\n- PII protection mechanisms (or lack thereof)\n- Encryption implementations