[2026-04-08 07:50:50] [injection-vuln] [LLM] Turn 152: {"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have all the analysis I need. Let me compile the findings and update the todo list. Let me think about what the projects.service.js finding actually means:\n\nThe agent said:\n```javascript\nthis.where('projects.title', 'ilike', `%${q}%`)\n```\n\nThis uses Knex's three-argument `.where(column, operator, value)` form. The template literal `%${q}%` creates a JavaScript string. Knex then takes this string and uses it as a bound parameter. So:\n- The SQL generated would be: `WHERE \"projects\".\"title\" ilike $1` with `$1 = '%userInput%'`\n\n\n- This is properly parameterized by Knex - the `%${q}%` is just a JavaScript string concatenation to add the wildcards, which is different from vulnerable patterns like `whereRaw(\"title ILIKE '%\" + search + \"%'\")` or directly embedding the search in a raw SQL string. Since Knex handles the binding, this is actually safe - the agent incorrectly flagged it as a false positive.\n\nNow I'm compiling my final findings: the SQLi vulnerability in users.service.js at line 33 with the `%${search}%` pattern in raw SQL concatenation is the primary concern, along with the admin.service.js issue at line 66 using whereRaw with string concatenation. Both are high-confidence, externally exploitable vulnerabilities. Let me finalize the deliverables with these confirmed findings. )\n3. INJ-VULN-03: NoSQLi PUBLIC - gigs.service.js:41-45 - $where with template literal → VULNERABLE but potentially blocked by MongoDB 7 policy, MED confidence\n4. INJ-VULN-04: NoSQLi ADMIN - admin.service.js:358-364 - JSON string concat + parse → VULNERABLE, HIGH confidence, externally exploitable (JWT forgeable)\n5. INJ-VULN-05: SSTI/HTML-Inject Puppeteer - pdf.js:55-76 - user data in HTML template → VULNERABLE, HIGH confidence, externally exploitable (second-order)\n6. INJ-VULN-06: SSRF - integrations/import?url= - no restrictions → VULNERABLE, HIGH confidence\n7. INJ-VULN-07: SSRF - webhooks/test url - no restrictions → VULNERABLE, HIGH confidence\n8. INJ-VULN-08: SSRF - link-preview url - partial blocklist → VULNERABLE, HIGH confidence (bypass available)\n\nI'm also noting the safe findings where parameterized queries are properly used across several services, and flagging sources 9-12 as out of scope for injection analysis since they cover HTML injection in emails, stored XSS, file upload issues, and business logic flaws that fall outside the injection vulnerability queue. they're not SQLi/CMDi/SSTI/etc. Let me reconsider:\n\nActually, the queue format includes LFI, RFI, SSTI, PathTraversal, InsecureDeserialization. The Puppeteer HTML injection is SSTI. The file upload could be LFI-adjacent. Let me think about what to include.\n\nFor my scope as Injection Analysis Specialist:\n- Source 9 (Email HTML injection): Not really an injection attack in the traditional sense - it's HTML injection into email. I'll document as out-of-scope for injection queue but note it.\n- Source 10 (Stored XSS): Client-side XSS - not in my injection scope\n- Source 11 (File upload): Uploading HTML/SVG with scripts is XSS, not LFI/RFI\n- Source 12 (Webhook bypass): Business logic, not injection\n\nSo I'm narrowing down to the core injection vulnerabilities: the two SQLi issues in search endpoints, the NoSQLi in tag filtering and admin activity, and the SSTI in the PDF generation. I'll structure these as a prioritized queue with externally exploitable status.\n\nNow I need to think about whether the admin-level vulnerabilities are truly externally exploitable. The recon report flagged that the JWT secret is hardcoded as 'hireflow2024api', which means an attacker can forge admin tokens without needing legitimate credentials. That makes both the admin SQLi and admin NoSQLi externally exploitable despite the admin role requirement.\n\nTime to compile the final deliverables.","signature":""}
