[2026-04-08 07:57:50] [auth-vuln] [LLM] Turn 44: {"type":"thinking","thinking":"Key findings from the login response:\n\n1. **No authLimiter applied to auth routes** - Only `apiLimiter` is imported and applied globally to `/api`. The `authLimiter` is defined in `rateLimiter.js` but never imported or used in `auth.routes.js` or `index.js`. The global limiter allows 1000 requests per 15 minutes in development mode (when `NODE_ENV=development`).\n\n2. **Session cookie missing Secure flag and SameSite** - The `connect.sid` cookie is set with `HttpOnly` but:\n   - No `Secure` flag (since `config.env !== 'production'`)\n   - No `SameSite` attribute\n   - This means the session is sent over plain HTTP (no HTTPS)\n\n3. **JWT returned in response body** - The JWT is in the JSON response body (not a cookie), so client-side JS can access it. Not directly an auth flaw on the server side.\n\n4. **HSTS sent over HTTP** - The `Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=15552000; includeSubDomains` header is being sent over plain HTTP (port 3000, no TLS), which is ineffective.\n\n5. **Transport** - The application runs over HTTP only, which means all credentials and session data travel unencrypted.\n\n6. **Session fixation** - The login handler sets the user ID on the session without calling `req.session.regenerate()`, leaving it vulnerable to session fixation attacks. I should also examine the auth bypass vulnerability, password reset token reuse, and whether there are any default credentials in the system.","signature":""}
