[2026-04-08 08:02:03] [auth-vuln] [LLM] Turn 82: {"type":"thinking","thinking":"Looking at the `updatePassword` function:\n\n```javascript\nasync function updatePassword(userId, newPassword) {\n  const password_hash = await bcrypt.hash(newPassword, SALT_ROUNDS);\n  return db('users')\n    .where({ id: userId })\n    .update({\n      password_hash,\n      updated_at: new Date()\n    });\n}\n```\n\nThis does NOT:\n1. Clear `reset_token` field\n2. Clear `reset_token_expires` field\n\nThis means after a successful password reset:\n- The reset token remains in the DB\n\n\n- `findByResetToken(token)` would still find the user\n- The token is reusable within that 24-hour window\n\nThere's a timestamp check in the controller that validates tokens are less than 24 hours old, but the database also stores a `reset_token_expires` field with a 1-hour expiration—so there's a mismatch between the two validation mechanisms.\n\nLooking closer, `findByResetToken` only checks the token itself, never the `reset_token_expires` field in the database. The controller's timestamp check allows 24 hours, but the database field suggests 1 hour. And critically, `updatePassword` doesn't clear the reset token after use, so the same token can be reused multiple times within that 24-hour window. This is a significant security vulnerability.\n\nThe token generation uses a timestamp plus a truncated SHA256 hash of the email and timestamp, which gives about 64 bits of entropy—reasonable against brute force but potentially predictable if an attacker knows the approximate time. I'll set that aside for now and focus on the confirmed flaws.\n\nI should also check whether MailHog is externally accessible on port 8025, since that's where password reset emails are being sent.","signature":""}
