[2026-04-08 08:07:22] [auth-vuln] [LLM] Turn 93: {"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01DjQhrESmj4e7gopJs7DPnS","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"save-deliverable --type AUTH_QUEUE --content '{\n  \"vulnerabilities\": [\n    {\n      \"ID\": \"AUTH-VULN-01\",\n      \"vulnerability_type\": \"Transport_Exposure\",\n      \"externally_exploitable\": true,\n      \"source_endpoint\": \"POST /api/auth/login, POST /api/auth/register, GET /api/auth/me, ALL auth endpoints\",\n      \"vulnerable_code_location\": \"src/index.js (no TLS config); nginx/default.conf (HTTP only port 80); Strict-Transport-Security header sent over plain HTTP at src/index.js:50-53 (helmet config)\",\n      \"missing_defense\": \"No HTTPS/TLS at any layer (Nginx port 80, Express port 3000 both HTTP). The HSTS header (max-age=15552000) is transmitted over plain HTTP and is therefore completely ineffective — browsers only enforce HSTS received over HTTPS. All credentials, JWTs, and session cookies traverse the network in cleartext.\",\n      \"exploitation_hypothesis\": \"An attacker with network interception capability (e.g., same LAN, rogue AP, or malicious ISP node) can capture plaintext login credentials (email + password) and session cookies or JWT tokens from in-flight HTTP requests, then replay them to authenticate as the victim user.\",\n      \"suggested_exploit_technique\": \"session_hijacking\",\n      \"confidence\": \"High\",\n      \"notes\": \"Both port 80 (Nginx) and port 3000 (Express direct) are HTTP-only. The session cookie lacks Secure flag (see AUTH-VULN-08), making it transmittable over HTTP. Every authenticated API call leaks the JWT in the Authorization header or the connect.sid in Cookie header in cleartext.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"ID\": \"AUTH-VULN-02\",\n      \"vulnerability_type\": \"Token_Management_Issue\",\n      \"externally_exploitable\": true,\n      \"source_endpoint\": \"POST /api/auth/logout\",\n      \"vulnerable_code_location\": \"src/auth/auth.controller.js:110-123 (logout destroys session only); src/auth/auth.service.js:79-82 (JWT signed with 7d TTL, no jti or revocation)\",\n      \"missing_defense\": \"Logout only destroys the server-side Redis session via req.session.destroy(). The JWT Bearer token issued at login continues to be accepted by the authenticate middleware (src/middleware/auth.js:21-37) for its full 7-day lifetime. No JWT blacklist, deny-list, or jti tracking exists anywhere in the codebase.\",\n      \"exploitation_hypothesis\": \"An attacker who obtains a victim JWT (via network interception, XSS, or shoulder-surfing) can continue to authenticate as the victim indefinitely (up to 7 days) even after the victim explicitly logs out, bypassing the logout security control entirely.\",\n      \"suggested_exploit_technique\": \"token_replay\",\n      \"confidence\": \"High\",\n      \"notes\": \"CONFIRMED LIVE: JWT token used against GET /api/auth/me returned HTTP 200 with full user profile after POST /api/auth/logout returned success. JWT payload includes { id, email, role, walletBalance } — all seven-day-valid credentials for any authenticated API call.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"ID\": \"AUTH-VULN-03\",\n      \"vulnerability_type\": \"Abuse_Defenses_Missing\",\n      \"externally_exploitable\": true,\n      \"source_endpoint\": \"POST /api/auth/login, POST /api/auth/register, POST /api/auth/forgot-password, POST /api/auth/reset-password\",\n      \"vulnerable_code_location\": \"src/middleware/rateLimiter.js:13-19 (authLimiter defined but unused); src/index.js:22 (only apiLimiter imported); src/auth/auth.routes.js (no rate limit middleware applied)\",\n      \"missing_defense\": \"The authLimiter (20 req/15 min) is defined but never imported or applied. All four authentication endpoints are protected only by the global apiLimiter which allows 1,000 requests per 15-minute window in development mode (NODE_ENV=development confirmed). No account lockout, exponential backoff, or CAPTCHA exists anywhere in the codebase.\",\n      \"exploitation_hypothesis\": \"An attacker can submit up to 1,000 login attempts per 15 minutes without triggering any throttle or lockout, enabling brute-force of user passwords or credential stuffing with leaked password lists. Combined with weak bcrypt (4 rounds, AUTH-VULN-09), password cracking is extremely fast both online and offline.\",\n      \"suggested_exploit_technique\": \"brute_force_login\",\n      \"confidence\": \"High\",\n      \"notes\": \"CONFIRMED LIVE via RateLimit-Policy: 1000;w=900 response header on POST /api/auth/login and POST /api/auth/forgot-password. Environment is development (per /api/debug/info) so production limit of 200/15min also applies to login — still insufficient. No CAPTCHA, no lockout. Try common passwords against enumerated users (user enumeration available via AUTH-VULN-07).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"ID\": \"AUTH-VULN-04\",\n      \"vulnerability_type\": \"Reset_Recovery_Flaw\",\n      \"externally_exploitable\": true,\n      \"source_endpoint\": \"POST /api/auth/forgot-password (triggers email); GET http://host.docker.internal:8025/api/v2/messages (MailHog API)\",\n      \"vulnerable_code_location\": \"src/config/index.js:43-46 (SMTP config pointing to MailHog); src/utils/helpers.js:5-8 (nodemailer transporter with ignoreTLS: true); docker-compose.yml (mailhog service with ports 1025:1025, 8025:8025 exposed, no auth)\",\n      \"missing_defense\": \"MailHog is a development email relay that captures all outbound email in memory. Its web UI and REST API (port 8025) are exposed on all host interfaces with zero authentication. All password reset tokens and email verification tokens for all users are readable via a single unauthenticated HTTP GET.\",\n      \"exploitation_hypothesis\": \"An attacker can take over any user account by: (1) calling POST /api/auth/forgot-password with the target email, (2) immediately fetching GET http://host.docker.internal:8025/api/v2/messages to read the reset token, (3) calling POST /api/auth/reset-password with the stolen token and an attacker-controlled password — achieving full account takeover in three requests.\",\n      \"suggested_exploit_technique\": \"reset_token_guessing\",\n      \"confidence\": \"High\",\n      \"notes\": \"CONFIRMED LIVE: Retrieved active reset tokens for multiple users including audituser@test.com (tokens: mnprf0ae-8e2463a34cc7cc69, mnpra6hb-abd1d9a03fde2826) and testclient@hireflow.com (mnpng3cy-48091580948362f9, mnpndvri-e87ef5934f152f34) via single unauthenticated request to MailHog API. Token format: {timestamp_base36}-{sha256(email+timestamp)[:16]}. Chained with AUTH-VULN-05 (reusable tokens) for repeat access.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"ID\": \"AUTH-VULN-05\",\n      \"vulnerability_type\": \"Reset_Recovery_Flaw\",\n      \"externally_exploitable\": true,\n      \"source_endpoint\": \"POST /api/auth/reset-password\",\n      \"vulnerable_code_location\": \"src/auth/auth.service.js:131-138 (updatePassword does not clear reset_token); src/auth/auth.controller.js:180-184 (checks tokenAge > 86400000 = 24h, not 1h); src/auth/auth.service.js:126-129 (findByResetToken does not filter by reset_token_expires)\",\n      \"missing_defense\": \"Two compounding flaws: (1) updatePassword() sets only password_hash — never nullifies reset_token or reset_token_expires columns, allowing the same token to be reused for unlimited password resets within the expiry window. (2) The effective expiry window is 24 hours (86,400,000 ms checked in controller), not 1 hour as stated in the email and stored in reset_token_expires (which is never queried).\",\n      \"exploitation_hypothesis\": \"An attacker who obtains a reset token (via AUTH-VULN-04 MailHog or AUTH-VULN-06 Host Header Injection) can use it to reset the password, then use the same token again to reset it a second time — locking the legitimate user out permanently. The 24-hour window also gives the attacker ample time to act without urgency.\",\n      \"suggested_exploit_technique\": \"reset_token_guessing\",\n      \"confidence\": \"High\",\n      \"notes\": \"Code confirmed: updatePassword() at auth.service.js:131-138 has no reset_token: null in the update object. findByResetToken() at auth.service.js:126-129 uses only .where({ reset_token: token }) with no expiry check. The DB column reset_token_expires is write-only. Controller at auth.controller.js:184 uses 86400000 (24h) despite the email and service comment saying 1 hour.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"ID\": \"AUTH-VULN-06\",\n      \"vulnerability_type\": \"Reset_Recovery_Flaw\",\n      \"externally_exploitable\": true,\n      \"source_endpoint\": \"POST /api/auth/forgot-password\",\n      \"vulnerable_code_location\": \"src/auth/auth.controller.js:141\",\n      \"missing_defense\": \"The password reset URL is constructed using req.get('host') without validation: `${req.protocol}://${req.get('host')}/reset-password?token=${resetToken}`. An attacker who controls the Host request header can cause the application to send a password reset email containing a link pointing to an attacker-controlled domain, where the token is surrendered when the victim clicks the link.\",\n      \"exploitation_hypothesis\": \"An attacker sends POST /api/auth/forgot-password with a victim email and a spoofed Host header (e.g., Host: attacker.com). The application sends the victim a password reset email with a link to http://attacker.com/reset-password?token=<valid_token>. When the victim clicks the link, the token is transmitted to the attacker's server, enabling account takeover.\",\n      \"suggested_exploit_technique\": \"reset_token_guessing\",\n      \"confidence\": \"High\",\n      \"notes\": \"CONFIRMED LIVE: MailHog contains email to testclient@hireflow.com with reset URL http://evil.com/reset-password?token=mnpndvri-e87ef5934f152f34 — proving Host header injection works. Attack does not require MITM — only a forged HTTP request header. Effective even when MailHog is not accessible externally.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"ID\": \"AUTH-VULN-07\",\n      \"vulnerability_type\": \"Login_Flow_Logic\",\n      \"externally_exploitable\": true,\n      \"source_endpoint\": \"POST /api/auth/forgot-password\",\n      \"vulnerable_code_location\": \"src/auth/auth.controller.js:134-136\",\n      \"missing_defense\": \"POST /api/auth/forgot-password returns HTTP 404 with message 'No account found with that email address' for unregistered emails, vs HTTP 200 with 'Password reset link sent to your email' for registered emails. This allows an attacker to confirm whether any email address has a registered account.\",\n      \"exploitation_hypothesis\": \"An attacker can enumerate all registered user email addresses by submitting a list of candidate emails to POST /api/auth/forgot-password and observing the HTTP status code differential (404 = not registered, 200 = registered). This enables targeted brute-force (AUTH-VULN-03) and social engineering attacks against confirmed accounts.\",\n      \"suggested_exploit_technique\": \"account_enumeration\",\n      \"confidence\": \"High\",\n      \"notes\": \"CONFIRMED LIVE: POST with audituser@test.com returns 200 + 'Password reset link sent to your email'. POST with nonexistent99999@test.com returns 404 + 'No account found with that email address'. No rate limiting on this endpoint (AUTH-VULN-03). Combined with AUTH-VULN-04 and AUTH-VULN-03 forms a complete reconnaissance-to-takeover chain.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"ID\": \"AUTH-VULN-08\",\n      \"vulnerability_type\": \"Session_Management_Flaw\",\n      \"externally_exploitable\": true,\n      \"source_endpoint\": \"ALL authenticated endpoints using Cookie: connect.sid\",\n      \"vulnerable_code_location\": \"src/index.js:61-70 (session config: secure conditional on production, no sameSite)\",\n      \"missing_defense\": \"Session cookie missing two security attributes: (1) Secure flag is absent because config.env !== 'production' (environment is 'development') — the cookie is transmitted over plaintext HTTP. (2) SameSite attribute is not configured at all — the cookie will be sent on cross-site requests, enabling CSRF-based session abuse. CORS policy reflects any origin with credentials (src/index.js:46-49), amplifying the SameSite omission.\",\n      \"exploitation_hypothesis\": \"An attacker on the same network can intercept the plaintext session cookie from HTTP traffic and replay it to authenticate as the victim. Separately, the missing SameSite attribute allows cross-origin requests to carry the session cookie, enabling CSRF attacks against state-changing endpoints.\",\n      \"suggested_exploit_technique\": \"session_hijacking\",\n      \"confidence\": \"High\",\n      \"notes\": \"CONFIRMED LIVE: Login response Set-Cookie header: 'connect.sid=...; Path=/; HttpOnly' — no Secure, no SameSite. CORS: 'origin: true, credentials: true' at src/index.js:46-49 reflects any origin. The Secure flag absence is particularly impactful given the HTTP-only transport (AUTH-VULN-01).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"ID\": \"AUTH-VULN-09\",\n      \"vulnerability_type\": \"Abuse_Defenses_Missing\",\n      \"externally_exploitable\": true,\n      \"source_endpoint\": \"POST /api/auth/register, POST /api/auth/login (password verification path)\",\n      \"vulnerable_code_location\": \"src/auth/auth.service.js:8 (SALT_ROUNDS = 4)\",\n      \"missing_defense\": \"bcrypt work factor is set to 4 rounds with an inline comment acknowledging this is intentionally low 'for faster signups during the beta'. OWASP recommends a minimum of 12 rounds. At 4 rounds, bcrypt is approximately 256x faster than at 12 rounds, making offline cracking of leaked PostgreSQL hashes trivial with commodity GPU hardware.\",\n      \"exploitation_hypothesis\": \"If password hashes are obtained (e.g., via SQL injection on the exposed /api/users?search= or /api/admin/users?search= endpoints, or direct PostgreSQL access on exposed port 5432), an attacker can crack the 4-round bcrypt hashes in