gin: *' (src/config/socket.js:8-11). The messaging namespace (/messaging) is dead code per recon but the default namespace is fully active and unauthenticated.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"ID\": \"AUTH-VULN-11\",\n      \"vulnerability_type\": \"Authentication_Bypass\",\n      \"externally_exploitable\": true,\n      \"source_endpoint\": \"ALL endpoints protected by authenticate middleware\",\n      \"vulnerable_code_location\": \"src/middleware/auth.js:31-36\",\n      \"missing_defense\": \"In the JWT verification try/catch block, only JsonWebTokenError and TokenExpiredError are handled with a 401 response. Any other exception (e.g., database connection failure, query timeout, unexpected runtime error during db('users').where().first()) falls through to 'return next()' at line 36 — advancing the request pipeline with req.user === undefined. Routes that do not defensively null-check req.user before use could process the request as if authenticated.\",\n      \"exploitation_hypothesis\": \"An attacker who can trigger a transient database error (e.g., by saturating PostgreSQL connections or exploiting a timing window during DB restart) while sending a request with any Bearer token could cause the authenticate middleware to call next() without setting req.user, potentially bypassing authentication on susceptible route handlers.\",\n      \"suggested_exploit_technique\": \"session_hijacking\",\n      \"confidence\": \"Medium\",\n      \"notes\": \"The flaw is deterministic in code but requires an external trigger (DB error) to manifest. Practical exploitability depends on the attacker's ability to cause DB-level errors. However, under sustained DoS on the DB layer, any request with a syntactically valid Bearer token (even random bytes that don't parse as a JWT error) would pass through. Medium confidence due to required precondition.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"ID\": \"AUTH-VULN-12\",\n      \"vulnerability_type\": \"Token_Management_Issue\",\n      \"externally_exploitable\