dleware for its full 7-day lifetime. No JWT blacklist, deny-list, or jti tracking exists anywhere in the codebase.\",\n      \"exploitation_hypothesis\": \"An attacker who obtains a victim JWT can continue to authenticate as the victim for up to 7 days even after the victim explicitly logs out, bypassing the logout security control entirely.\",\n      \"suggested_exploit_technique\": \"token_replay\",\n      \"confidence\": \"High\",\n      \"notes\": \"CONFIRMED LIVE: JWT used against GET /api/auth/me returned HTTP 200 with full user profile immediately after POST /api/auth/logout returned success. JWT payload includes id, email, role, walletBalance — all valid for 7 days.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"ID\": \"AUTH-VULN-03\",\n      \"vulnerability_type\": \"Abuse_Defenses_Missing\",\n      \"externally_exploitable\": true,\n      \"source_endpoint\": \"POST /api/auth/login\",\n      \"vulnerable_code_location\": \"src/middleware/rateLimiter.js:13-19 (authLimiter defined but unused); src/index.js:22 (only apiLimiter imported); src/auth/auth.routes.js (no rate limit middleware applied to any route)\",\n      \"missing_defense\": \"The authLimiter (20 req/15 min) is defined but never imported or applied. All auth endpoints are subject only to the global apiLimiter which allows 1000 requests per 15 minutes in development mode. No account lockout, backoff, or CAPTCHA exists anywhere.\",\n      \"exploitation_hypothesis\": \"An attacker can submit up to 1000 login attempts per 15 minutes without triggering any throttle or lockout, enabling brute-force of user passwords or credential stuffing with leaked password lists.\",\n      \"suggested_exploit_technique\": \"brute_force_login\",\n      \"confidence\": \"High\",\n      \"notes\": \"CONFIRMED LIVE via RateLimit-Policy: 1000;w=900 header on POST /api/auth/login and POST /api/auth/forgot-password. Applies equally to /register and /reset-password. NODE_ENV=development confirmed via /api/debug/info. No CAPTCHA, no lockout. Use enumerated emails from AUTH-VULN-07 as targets.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"ID\": \"AUTH-VULN-04\",\n      \"vulnerability_type\": \"Reset_Recovery_Flaw\",\n      \"externally_exploitable\": true,\n      \"source_endpoint\": \"POST /api/auth/forgot-password\",\n      \"vulnerable_code_location\": \"src/config/index.js:43-46 (SMTP config pointing to MailHog); src/utils/helpers.js:5-8 (nodemailer transporter); docker-compose.yml mailhog service (ports 8025:8025 exposed, no auth)\",\n      \"missing_defense\": \"MailHog development email relay (port 8025) is exposed on all host interfaces with zero authentication. All password reset tokens and email verification tokens for all users are readable via a single unauthenticated HTTP GET to the MailHog REST API.\",\n      \"exploitation_hypothesis\": \"An attacker can take over any user account by triggering POST /api/auth/forgot-password with the target email, then fetching GET http://host.docker.internal:8025/api/v2/messages to read the reset token, then calling POST /api/auth/reset-password to set an attacker-controlled password — achieving full account takeover in three requests.\",\n      \"suggested_exploit_technique\": \"reset_token_guessing\",\n      \"confidence\": \"High\",\n      \"notes\": \"CONFIRMED LIVE: Retrieved active reset tokens for multiple users via single unauthenticated request to MailHog API. Tokens confirmed for audituser@test.com and testclient@hireflow.com. Chained with AUTH-VULN-05 (reusable tokens with 24h window) for persistent access.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"ID\": \"AUTH-VULN-05\",\n      \"vulnerability_type\": \"Reset_Recovery_Flaw\",\n      \"externally_exploitable\": true,\n      \"source_endpoint\": \"POST /api/auth/reset-password\",\n      \"vulnerable_code_location\": \"src/auth/auth.service.js:131-138 (updatePassword does not clear reset_token); src/auth/auth.controller.js:180-184 (checks tokenAge > 86400000 = 24h not 1h); src/auth/auth.service.js:126-129 (findByResetToken does not filter by reset_token_expires)\",\n      \"missing_defense\": \"Two compounding flaws: updatePassword() never nullifies reset_token or reset_token_expires columns, allowing the same token to be reused for unlimited password resets within the expiry window. The effective expiry window is 24 hours (86400000 ms in controller) not 1 hour as claimed in the email. The reset_token_expires DB column is stored but never queried.\",\n      \"exploitation_hypothesis\": \"An attacker who obtains a reset token can use it to reset the password, then use the same token again to reset it a second time, permanently locking the legitimate user out. The 24-hour window gives ample time for multi-stage attack.\",\n      \"suggested_exploit_technique\": \"reset_token_guessing\",\n      \"confidence\": \"High\",\n      \"notes\": \"Code confirmed: updatePassword() at auth.service.js:131-138 has no reset_token nullification. findByResetToken() uses only .where({ reset_token: token }) with no expiry check. Controller at auth.controller.js:184 uses 86400000 (24h). DB stores reset_token_expires but it is write-only.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"ID\": \"AUTH-VULN-06\",\n      \"vulnerability_type\": \"Reset_Recovery_Flaw\",\n      \"externally_exploitable\": true,\n      \"source_endpoint\": \"POST /api/auth/forgot-password\",\n      \"vulnerable_code_location\": \"src/auth/auth.controller.js:141\",\n      \"missing_defense\": \"The password reset URL is constructed using req.get('host') without validation. An attacker who controls the Host request header can cause the application to generate a password reset email containing a link pointing to an attacker-controlled domain.\",\n      \"exploitation_hypothesis\": \"An attacker sends POST /api/auth/forgot-password with a victim email and a spoofed Host header (e.g., Host: attacker.com). The application sends the victim a reset email with a link to http://attacker.com/reset-password?token=valid_token. When the victim clicks the link, the token is transmitted to the attacker's server, enabling account takeover.\",\n      \"suggested_exploit_technique\": \"reset_token_guessing\",\n      \"confidence\": \"High\",\n      \"notes\": \"CONFIRMED LIVE: MailHog contains email to testclient@hireflow.com with reset URL http://evil.com/reset-password?token=mnpndvri-e87ef5934f152f34 proving Host header injection works. Attack does not require MITM. Only requires a forged HTTP Host header in the forgot-password request.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"ID\": \"AUTH-VULN-07\",\n      \"vulnerability_type\": \"Login_Flow_Logic\",\n      \"externally_exploitable\": true,\n      \"source_endpoint\": \"POST /api/auth/forgot-password\",\n      \"vulnerable_code_location\": \"src/auth/auth.controller.js:134-136\",\n      \"missing_defense\": \"Forgot-password endpoint returns HTTP 404 with message 'No account found with that email address' for unregistered emails vs HTTP 200 with 'Password reset link sent to your email' for registered emails. The HTTP status code difference allows email enumeration.\",\n      \"exploitation_hypothesis\": \"An attacker can confirm whether any email address has a registered account, enabling targeted brute-force against confirmed users and reducing the search space for credential stuffing attacks.\",\n      \"suggested_exploit_technique\": \"account_enumeration\",\n      \"confidence\": \"High\",\n      \"notes\": \"CONFIRMED LIVE: audituser@test.com returns 200, nonexistent99999@test.com returns 404. No rate limiting on this endpoint (AUTH-VULN-03). Enables efficient targeted attack: enumerate accounts first, then brute-force or use reset-chain takeover via AUTH-VULN-04.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"ID\": \"AUTH-VULN-08\",\n      \"vulnerability_type\": \"Session_Management_Flaw\",\n      \"externally_exploitable\": true,\n      \"source_endpoint\": \"ALL authenticated endpoints using Cookie: connect.sid\",\n      \"vulnerable_code_location\": \"src/index.js:61-70 (session config: secure conditional on production, no sameSite configured)\",\n      \"missing_defense\": \"Session cookie missing two security attributes: Secure flag absent because NODE_ENV is development; SameSite attribute not configured at all. The CORS policy reflects any origin with credentials (origin: true, credentials: true at src/index.js:46-49), amplifying the missing SameSite.\",\n      \"exploitation_hypothesis\": \"An attacker on the same network can intercept the plaintext session cookie from HTTP traffic and replay it to authenticate as the victim. The missing SameSite attribute allows cross-origin requests to carry the session cookie, enabling CSRF-based session abuse.\",\n      \"suggested_exploit_technique\": \"session_hijacking\",\n      \"confidence\": \"High\",\n      \"notes\": \"CONFIRMED LIVE: Login Set-Cookie header: 'connect.sid=...; Path=/; HttpOnly' with no Secure and no SameSite. HTTP-only transport (AUTH-VULN-01) makes Secure flag absence directly exploitable. Wildcard CORS with credentials enabled further expands attack surface.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"ID\": \"AUTH-VULN-09\",\n      \"vulnerability_type\": \"Abuse_Defenses_Missing\",\n      \"externally_exploitable\": true,\n      \"source_endpoint\": \"POST /api/auth/register\",\n      \"vulnerable_code_location\": \"src/auth/auth.service.js:8\",\n      \"missing_defense\": \"bcrypt work factor set to 4 rounds (SALT_ROUNDS = 4) with comment acknowledging this is intentionally low for beta performance. OWASP recommends minimum 12 rounds. At 4 rounds bcrypt is 256x faster than at 12 rounds, making offline cracking of leaked hashes trivial with GPU hardware.\",\n      \"exploitation_hypothesis\": \"If password hashes are obtained via SQL injection on exposed endpoints or direct PostgreSQL access on exposed port 5432, an attacker can crack the 4-round bcrypt hashes in minutes using hashcat, enabling full credential recovery for all users.\",\n      \"suggested_exploit_technique\": \"credential_stuffing\",\n      \"confidence\": \"High\",\n      \"notes\": \"Code confirmed at src/auth/auth.service.js:8. Force-multiplier for SQLi findings — hash exfiltration plus rapid offline crack equals full account list compromise. No password complexity requirements beyond 8-character minimum increases proportion of crackable passwords.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"ID\": \"AUTH-VULN-10\",\n      \"vulnerability_type\": \"Session_Management_Flaw\",\n      \"externally_exploitable\": true,\n      \"source_endpoint\": \"ws://host.docker.internal:3000/socket.io\",\n      \"vulnerable_code_location\": \"src/config/socket.js:18 (userId from query param without verification); src/config/socket.js:35 (typing event userId from client payload)\",\n      \"missing_defense\": \"Socket.IO server reads userId from client-supplied query parameter with no session cookie or JWT verification during the handshake. Any WebSocket client can claim any userId. The typing event further takes userId from the client event payload making it doubly spoofable.\",\n      \"exploitation_hypothesis\": \"An attacker can connect to the WebSocket endpoint claiming to be any user, receive real-time events destined for that user, join any conversation room, and inject typing presence indicators impersonating any user — all without valid credentials.\",\n      \"suggested_exploit_technique\": \"session_hijacking\",\n      \"confidence\": \"High\",\n      \"notes\": \"Code at src/config/socket.js:18-23: userId stored in onlineUsers map and broadcast to all clients without authentication. CORS for Socket.IO is origin: '*'. Join conversation has no membership check. Default namespace is fully active and unauthenticated per recon.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"ID\": \"AUTH-VULN-11\",\n      \"vulnerability_type\": \"Authentication_Bypass\",\n      \"externally_exploitable\": true,\n      \"source_endpoint\": \"ALL endpoints protected by authenticate middleware\",\n      \"vulnerable_code_location\": \"src/middleware/auth.js:35-36\",\n      \"missing_defense\": \"In the JWT verification catch block, only JsonWebTokenError and TokenExpiredError are handled with a 401 response. Any other exception during the DB lookup after JWT verification falls through to return next() at line 36 with req.user undefined, potentially bypassing authentication on route handlers that do not null-check req.user.\",\n      \"exploitation_hypothesis\": \"An attacker who can trigger a transient database error while sending a request with any syntactically valid Bearer token string could cause the authenticate middleware to call next() without setting req.user, bypassing authentication on susceptible route handlers.\",\n      \"suggested_exploit_technique\": \"session_hijacking\",\n      \"confidence\": \"Medium\",\n      \"notes\": \"Flaw is deterministic in code but requires external trigger (DB error). Practical exploitability depends on ability to cause DB-level errors. Under sustained pressure on PostgreSQL connection pool, requests with any Bearer token string may pass through unauthenticated.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"ID\": \"AUTH-VULN-12\",\n      \"vulnerability_type\": \"Token_Management_Issue\",\n      \"externally_exploitable\": true,\n      \"source_endpoint\": \"ALL authenticated endpoints using JWT Bearer\",\n      \"vulnerable_code_location\": \"src/config/index.js:30\",\n      \"missing_defense\": \"JWT secret falls back to hardcoded value 'hireflow2024api' when JWT_SECRET environment variable is not set. No .env file exists on disk. If the runtime environment variable is unset, any attacker with knowledge of this public default can forge arbitrary JWTs with any role including superadmin.\",\n      \"exploitation_hypothesis\": \"If JWT_SECRET env var is unset, an attacker can forge a JWT signed with 'hireflow2024api' claiming role: 'superadmin' and arbitrary user ID, gaining unrestricted administrative access to all API endpoints including user role management and audit logs.\",\n      \"suggested_exploit_technique\": \"token_replay\",\n      \"confidence\": \"Medium\",\n      \"notes\": \"Live JWT signature verification with 'hireflow2024api' did not match observed token, suggesting JWT_SECRET env var may be set at container runtime via mechanism not visible on disk. However code-level fallback is confirmed. Test: forge HS256 JWT with secret 'hireflow2024api' and { id: valid_user_id, role: 'superadmin', exp: now+86400 } against GET /api/admin/dashboard.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"ID\": \"AUTH-VULN-13\",\n      \"vulnerability_type\": \"Login_Flow_Logic\",\n      \"externally_exploitable\": true,\n      \"source_endpoint\": \"POST /api/auth/login\",\n      \"vulnerable_code_location\": \"src/auth/auth.controller.js:94-96\",\n      \"missing_defense\": \"After successful password verification, login sets req.session.userId without calling req.session.regenerate(). If a session already exists, the same session ID is elevated to an authenticated session. saveUninitialized: false reduces practical risk but the missing regenerate() call is a structural flaw.\",\n      \"exploitation_hypothesis\": \"An attacker who pre-plants a known session ID in the victim browser (via network interception on HTTP) and then the victim logs in, the attacker retains access to the now-authenticated session using the pre-planted session ID without knowing the victim credentials.\",\n      \"suggested_exploit_technique\": \"session_fixation\",\n      \"confidence\": \"Low\",\n      \"notes\": \"Practical risk reduced by saveUninitialized: false meaning no session is issued before login under normal flow. However if any middleware initializes the session before login the fixation becomes exploitable. Low confidence for exploitation but code fix is trivial: add req.session.regenerate() before setting userId.\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}
