[2026-04-08 08:27:36] [ssrf-exploit] [LLM] Turn 106: {"type":"thinking","thinking":"The text in the PDF is rendered as vector paths, not as searchable text. This is typical for PDFs generated by Chromium/Puppeteer - they embed text as font glyphs.\n\nLet me try a different approach to prove SSRF-VULN-04:\n\n1. **Image embedding approach**: Set display_name to `<img src=\"http://minio:9001/styles/root-styles.css\" id=\"ssrf-img\">` and generate the invoice. If the CSS file is loaded and embedded as a resource, the PDF will be significantly larger.\n\n2. **Timing approach**: Compare PDF generation time when hitting an accessible vs non-accessible host.\n\n\n3. **Self-referencing approach**: Use the app's own API as the SSRF target via the 0.0.0.0 bypass, which would create a network request we can trace.\n\nActually, the simplest approach is to compare PDF sizes - a normal invoice as baseline, then one with an SSRF img tag pointing to MinIO to see if it loads and increases the file size, versus one pointing to a non-existent host which should timeout and stay at baseline size. Or I could generate a PDF with a script and check if the document title contains data from MinIO.\n\nLooking at the PDF title showing \"about:blank\" - that's because the invoice template doesn't have a `<title>` tag in the head section, so Puppeteer defaults to that. The JavaScript I injected was trying to fetch from MinIO and update a DOM element with the response, but the element content wouldn't show up in the PDF title itself.\n\nSince I can't easily inspect the rendered vector paths in the PDF, I need a different approach. Let me try injecting a title tag directly through the display_name parameter and have the script modify the document title with the MinIO response data - that way the title should appear in the PDF metadata and be readable.\n\nI realize synchronous XHR is deprecated and unreliable, so let me step back. I've already confirmed through the previous vulnerabilities that mini