aphic attack. The weak token design is an independent additional flaw. The DB expiry field (reset_token_expires) is stored but NEVER queried in findByResetToken(); expiry is checked only via the embedded timestamp in the token string.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"ID\": \"AUTH-VULN-08\",\n      \"vulnerability_type\": \"Reset_Recovery_Flaw\",\n      \"externally_exploitable\": true,\n      \"source_endpoint\": \"POST /api/auth/reset-password\",\n      \"vulnerable_code_location\": \"src/auth/auth.service.js:128-135 — updatePassword() only updates password_hash and updated_at; does NOT clear reset_token or reset_token_expires columns\",\n      \"missing_defense\": \"Used reset tokens are not cleared from the database after a successful password reset. The token remains valid and reusable for up to 24 hours after the legitimate reset operation.\",\n      \"exploitation_hypothesis\": \"An attacker who observes a password reset token (e.g., from MailHog, HTTP interception, or email logs) after the legitimate user has already used it can replay the same token to POST /api/auth/reset-password within the 24-hour window and set a new password, taking over the account.\",\n      \"suggested_exploit_technique\": \"reset_token_guessing\",\n      \"confidence\": \"High\",\n      \"notes\": \"The 24-hour expiry window is checked in the controller by extracting the timestamp from the token string (tokenAge > 86400000). The DB column reset_token_expires is stored but never used in any query. Single-use enforcement is completely absent.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"ID\": \"AUTH-VULN-09\",\n      \"vulnerability_type\": \"Transport_Exposure\",\n      \"externally_exploitable\": true,\n      \"source_endpoint\": \"ALL auth endpoints — POST /api/auth/login, POST /api/auth/register, POST /api/auth/forgot-password, POST /api/auth/reset-password\",\n      \"vulnerable_code_location\": \"Infrastructure — Nginx config (HTTP only, port 80); Express server direct on port 3000 (HTTP only); src/index.js:61-71 (secure: config.env === '\\''production'\\'' — false in deployment)\",\n      \"missing_defense\": \"No TLS/HTTPS configured anywhere in the stack. No HSTS header. No HTTP-to-HTTPS redirect. Session cookie `secure` flag is false (conditional on NODE_ENV=production which is not set). All credentials, session cookies, JWT tokens, and password reset links are transmitted in cleartext.\",\n      \"exploitation_hypothesis\": \"A network-level attacker (on the same network segment, or via ARP spoofing/rogue AP) passively intercepts POST /api/auth/login requests and extracts email/password credentials in cleartext, then uses them to authenticate as the victim.\",\n      \"suggested_exploit_technique\": \"session_hijacking\",\n      \"confidence\": \"High\",\n      \"notes\": \"Both Nginx (port 80) and direct Express (port 3000) are HTTP only. The `secure` cookie flag is gated on NODE_ENV=production, which is not set in the observed deployment, so session cookies are also sent over HTTP. Cache-Control headers on auth responses were not observed — no no-store/no-cache directives noted.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"ID\": \"AUTH-VULN-10\",\n      \"vulnerability_type\": \"Login_Flow_Logic\",\n      \"externally_exploitable\": true,\n      \"source_endpoint\": \"POST /api/auth/forgot-password\",\n      \"vulnerable_code_location\": \"src/auth/auth.controller.js:136-138\",\n      \"missing_defense\": \"Distinct 404 response body reveals account non-existence; registered accounts receive 200. Should return 200 with identical message regardless of account existence.\",\n      \"exploitation_hypothesis\": \"Already captured under AUTH-VULN-06 (account enumeration). Documented here as a Login_Flow_Logic classification for completeness.\",\n      \"suggested_exploit_technique\": \"account_enumeration\",\n      \"confidence\": \"High\",\n      \"notes\": \"Duplicate classification of AUTH-VULN-06 under Login_Flow_Logic type. Exploitation team should treat these as the same finding.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"ID\": \"AUTH-VULN-11\",\n      \"vulnerability_type\": \"Abuse_Defenses_Missing\",\n      \"externally_exploitable\": true,\n      \"source_endpoint\": \"POST /api/auth/register (credential creation) + POST /api/auth/login (credential verification)\",\n      \"vulnerable_code_location\": \"src/auth/auth.service.js:8 — `const SALT_ROUNDS = 4`; applied at line 11 (createUser) and line 129 (updatePassword)\",\n      \"missing_defense\": \"bcrypt work factor of 4 is far below the recommended minimum of 10-12. Passwords are ~64x easier to crack offline compared to 10-round bcrypt. No minimum password complexity requirements beyond 8-character length.\",\n      \"exploitation_hypothesis\": \"An attacker who obtains a dump of the users table (possible via SQL injection sinks at GET /api/users?search= and GET /api/admin/users?search=) performs offline dictionary/brute-force attacks against the bcrypt hashes at 4 rounds, recovering plaintext passwords at a significantly accelerated rate compared to standard configurations.\",\n      \"suggested_exploit_technique\": \"credential_stuffing\",\n      \"confidence\": \"High\",\n      \"notes\": \"Requires prior DB exfiltration (via SQLi or direct PostgreSQL access on port 5432). Work factor 4 means ~16 iterations per hash instead of ~1024 (round 10) — approximately 64x faster cracking. Combined with a minimum-8-char-only policy, weak/common passwords will crack quickly.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"ID\": \"AUTH-VULN-12\",\n      \"vulnerability_type\": \"Authentication_Bypass\",\n      \"externally_exploitable\": true,\n      \"source_endpoint\": \"WebSocket ws://host.docker.internal:3000/socket.io\",\n      \"vulnerable_code_location\": \"src/config/socket.js:18 — `const userId = socket.handshake.query.userId;` — no session/JWT validation\",\n      \"missing_defense\": \"Socket.IO default namespace accepts user identity (userId) directly from the client-supplied query parameter with no server-side verification against session or JWT. Any actor can claim to be any user.\",\n      \"exploitation_hypothesis\": \"An attacker connects to the Socket.IO endpoint with ?userId=<target_user_id> and is registered as that user in the server-side onlineUsers map. The server broadcasts user:online events attributing the attacker'\\''s connection to the target user, and the attacker can join any conversation room by sending join:conversation events, enabling real-time eavesdropping on messaging activity.\",\n      \"suggested_exploit_technique\": \"session_fixation\",\n      \"confidence\": \"High\",\n      \"notes\": \"User IDs are integer/UUID values obtainable from public API endpoints. The Socket.IO CORS policy is `origin: '\\''*'\\''` (wildcard), allowing connections from any domain. The typing event also accepts userId from the client payload (socket.js:35-37), allowing spoofed typing indicators attributed to arbitrary users.\"\n    }\n  ]\n}'","description":"Save auth exploitation queue"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}
[2026-04-08 08:28:11] [ssrf-exploit] [LLM] Turn 108: {"type":"thinking","thinking":"The PDF title is still \"about:blank\". This suggests that either:\n1. The synchronous XHR wasn't allowed in the headless Chrome context\n2. The title was set but then reset\n3. The HTML template includes a `<title>` tag that we need to check\n\nWait, looking at the template again - there's no `<title>` tag. Puppeteer uses `page.setContent(html, ...)` and the initial title is \"about:blank\" because that's the blank page's URL. The HTML we inject doesn't have a `<title>` tag, so Chrome keeps the default.\n\n\nThe script does set `document.title` via JavaScript, but if synchronous XHR is blocked in headless mode, that won't execute. I'm realizing the issue might be timing—the PDF could be rendering before the JavaScript finishes running, or the XHR request itself is being blocked by CORS even in headless Chrome. Let me trace through the Puppeteer flow to see where the title should be set. the HTML and WAITS for all network activity to stop\n2. `page.pdf(...)` - this takes the PDF snapshot\n\nSo if my script does `r.open('GET', 'http://minio:9001/', false); r.send()` (synchronous), this happens DURING the `setContent()` phase, which means the title SHOULD be set before `page.pdf()` is called.\n\nUnless Chrome disallows synchronous XHR from within content scripts loaded via `setContent()`.\n\nActually, synchronous XMLHttpRequest is not allowed in web workers, but should work in regular page context. However, modern Chrome has warnings about it.\n\nLet me check if there's a CORS issue - the page is loaded via `page.setContent()` which gives it a \"null\" origin or \"about:blank\" origin. XHR to `http://minio:9001/` from a null origin might be blocked by CORS. But wait, the async fetch I tried earlier would hit the same CORS restrictions, so the real issue could be that JavaScript fetches are being blocked entirely in headless Chrome.\n\nI should test whether an `<img>` tag pointing to MinIO would work instead, since images don't have the same CORS restrictions. If the PDF ends up larger or takes longer to generate, that would confirm the network request is actually happening. I also need to check if Puppeteer is running with `--disable-web-security` - looking at the args, I only see `['--no-sandbox', '--disable-setuid-sandbox']`, so CORS restrictions might still be enforced.\n\nSince `<img>` tags make simple GET requests without CORS preflight, an image source pointing to MinIO should trigger a network request even with these restrictions in place. Let me try that approach instead.","signature":""}
