[2026-04-08 08:30:41] [xss-vuln] [LLM] Turn 319: {"type":"thinking","thinking":"The invoice endpoint is `GET /api/contracts/:id/invoice`. This downloads a PDF. The attack here is:\n\n1. The attacker (as a client) registers with a malicious `display_name` containing HTML/script tags\n2. Creates a contract with a freelancer\n3. Requests the invoice for that contract\n4. The server builds the PDF using Puppeteer, which renders the HTML (including `${data.clientName}`)\n5. The `display_name` is interpolated directly into the HTML template without escaping\n\nHowever, Puppeteer generates a PDF, not an HTML page. The XSS doesn't execute in the victim's browser directly — the Puppeteer browser renders it server-side. This is actually:\n\n\n- **Server-Side HTML Injection / PDF Injection** (not strictly a browser XSS)\n- The impact could be: if the attacker can make the Puppeteer instance execute JS that reads server-side resources, it could be used for SSRF or data exfiltration\n\nThe real threat model is that a malicious `display_name` set during registration gets embedded in the invoice HTML template that Puppeteer renders. Since Puppeteer runs with `--no-sandbox`, there's potential for the injected content to access server-side resources or execute code in the rendering context.\n\nThe main risks are SSRF attacks through outbound requests made from the server, manipulation of PDF content to alter invoice details like amounts, and potentially reading files from the server if the injected script can access certain protocols.\n\nLooking at the actual vulnerability, this is really a Server-Side Template Injection or HTML Injection issue rather than traditional XSS, since Puppeteer is executing the JavaScript server-side. The unsanitized fields like `clientName`, `clientEmail`, and `description` are being directly interpolated into the HTML template without any escaping, which is the core problem. I should also check how email injection might be exploited in the contracts service.","signature":""}
