[2026-04-08 08:44:45] [auth-exploit] [LLM] Turn 142: {"type":"thinking","thinking":"The session fixation test shows that the server is NOT vulnerable to a direct session fixation attack because:\n1. Even though there's no `req.session.regenerate()` in the code, the empty/non-existent session creates a new session ID anyway\n2. The server creates a new session cookie on login regardless\n\nLooking at the code more carefully: `saveUninitialized: false` means the session isn't saved until something is set. But the key question is whether an EXISTING session gets its ID reused.\n\nLet me try a different approach:\n1. Create a session that ACTUALLY EXISTS in Redis (by logging in first)\n\n\n2. Then provide that session ID on a DIFFERENT login request\n3. Check if the session ID changes after the second login\n\nThis would test whether an attacker can fix a session by pre-creating one in Redis and forcing a victim to use it during login. The real vulnerability would be if the victim's login doesn't regenerate the session ID, allowing the attacker to maintain access through the same cookie.\n\nBut I'm realizing the mechanics here are tricky. If a session doesn't exist in Redis yet, express-session won't recognize the cookie and will just create a fresh session with a new ID during login. The only way session fixation could work is if there's already an existing session in Redis that the server reuses without regenerating. Since `saveUninitialized=false` means empty sessions aren't persisted, I need to test what happens when I try to log in with a session that's already been authenticated once before.","signature":""}
