[2026-04-08 08:45:40] [injection-exploit] [LLM] Turn 155: {"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01GhDcRAwaFnu76XBvANYxxG","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"mkdir -p /repos/webvulnbench/workspace\ncat > /repos/webvulnbench/workspace/injection_false_positives.md << 'EOF'\n# Injection False Positives Tracking\n\n## INJ-VULN-03: NoSQL $where Injection in GET /api/gigs?tag_filter=\n\n**Vulnerability Type:** NoSQL Injection ($where JavaScript execution)\n**Endpoint:** GET /api/gigs?tag_filter=\n**Date Tested:** 2026-04-08\n\n**What Was Attempted:**\n1. `tag_filter=true` - returned empty results (no users matching)\n2. `tag_filter=true || (function(){return true;})()` - returned `{\"error\": \"$where is not allowed in this context\"}`\n3. `tag_filter=true;sleep(3000);return true` - returned `{\"error\": \"$where is not allowed in this context\"}`\n\n**Root Cause:**\nThe code in `gigs.service.js:41-46` IS structurally vulnerable - it embeds user input directly into the `$where` JavaScript function body. However, MongoDB 7 ships with `javascriptEnabled: false` by default. The MongoDB server itself rejects `$where` queries with error: `\"$where is not allowed in this context\"`.\n\n**Why FALSE POSITIVE:**\nThe MongoDB `javascriptEnabled=false` configuration is a security control specifically designed to prevent JavaScript injection via `$where`. This is not an environmental issue or operational constraint - it IS the defense mechanism working as intended. All bypass attempts failed because the restriction is enforced at the database engine level, not the application layer.\n\n**Classification:** FALSE POSITIVE (database security configuration prevents exploitation)\nEOF\necho \"False positives file created\"","description":"Document INJ-VULN-03 as false positive"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}
