[2026-04-08 08:46:44] [auth-exploit] [LLM] Turn 153: {"type":"thinking","thinking":"AUTH-VULN-09 has an interesting result:\n- The app responds on HTTP port 3000 (no HTTPS)\n- HTTPS is not available (connection refused)\n- The session cookie lacks the `secure` flag (important finding!)\n- BUT Helmet has added `Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=15552000` (HSTS) - however this is pointless since there's no HTTPS\n\nActually wait - Helmet is setting `Strict-Transport-Security` but that's only useful if the site is actually served over HTTPS. Since there's no HTTPS, this header is on an HTTP response, which browsers ignore (HSTS headers on HTTP connections are ignored). So the situation is:\n\n\n- App runs on HTTP only with no TLS listener\n- Session cookie lacks the `secure` attribute, meaning credentials travel in cleartext\n- The HSTS header is ineffective since browsers ignore it on unencrypted connections\n\nFor AUTH-VULN-10, I'm looking at the bcrypt work factor - it's set to just 4 rounds, which hashes in about 11ms compared to the recommended 10 rounds at 57ms, giving attackers a significant speedup for brute force attempts.\n\nFor AUTH-VULN-01, the code has a hardcoded JWT secret fallback, but the running instance uses an environment variable with an unknown value, so I can't forge tokens for the live system even though the vulnerability exists in the codebase itself.\n\nFor AUTH-VULN-02, I've confirmed the session secret is the hardcoded default and I can forge cookies, but creating new arbitrary sessions would require Redis access which isn't exposed externally—though I did leverage the secret knowledge in the session fixation attack by working with existing sessions rather than injecting new ones.\n\nLet me verify the Socket.IO vulnerability more thoroughly before finalizing my findings, then I'll compile the complete summary of what's actually exploitable. I was able to connect to Socket.IO as the superadmin without any authentication and trigger user:online events for impersonated accounts. I also found user enumeration through different HTTP response codes for existing versus non-existent emails. There are additional vulnerabilities around unencrypted transport with missing security headers and weak bcrypt configuration that would require database access to fully exploit.\n\nThe JWT secret has a hardcoded fallback in the source code, but the live instance uses an environment variable I can't access, so I can't forge tokens there. For session cookies, I confirmed the secret is the hardcoded default and actually leveraged that knowledge in the session fixation attack—so while I can't directly inject sessions into Redis from outside, the vulnerability is confirmed and exploitable through the method I already demonstrated. Let me test the Socket.IO endpoints now.","signature":""}
